window.MONSTER_CHUNK_112 = [{"name":"Deadly Mandrake","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Tiny","type":"plant","init":4,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"tremorsense","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":17,"ac_touch":16,"ac_flat_footed":13,"ac_mods":"+4 Dex, +1 natural, +2 size","hp":16,"hd":"3d8+3","hdvalue":3,"fort":4,"ref":5,"will":5,"dr":"5/cold iron","immunities":["plant traits"],"sr":13,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30},{"type":"burrow","speed":10}],"melee":"2 claws +8 (1d2-2)","space":"1/2 ft.","reach":"0 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"death scream"}],"spell_like_abilities_caster_level":6,"spell_like_abilities_concentration":7,"spell_like_abilities":[{"frequency":"at-will","spells":[{"spell":"dancing lights"},{"spell":"ghost sound"},{"spell":"prestidigitation"}]},{"frequency":"1/day","spells":[{"spell":"hallucinatory terrain"},{"spell":"sleep"}]},{"frequency":"3/day","spells":[{"spell":"hypnotism"},{"spell":"lesser confusion"}]}],"strength":6,"dexterity":18,"constitution":13,"intelligence":10,"wisdom":13,"charisma":16,"baseatk":2,"cmb":33,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":47,"cmd_special":"+4 vs. trip","feats":[{"feat":"Alertness"},{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":5},{"skill":"Escape Artist","value":5},{"skill":"Knowledge (nature)","value":1},{"skill":"Perception","value":7},{"skill":"Perform (sing)","value":4},{"skill":"Sense Motive","value":3},{"skill":"Stealth","value":16,"special":"+24 in vegetation"}],"languages":["Common","Sylvan"],"environments":["temperate forests"],"organizations":["solitary","grove (2-6)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"This creature appears as a tiny humanoid, brownish-green in color with a large flowering plant growing from its head.","group":"Mandrake","source":"Tome of Horrors 4 (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Death Scream","kind":"Su","details":"Once per minute, a deadly mandrake may scream as a full-round action. The scream lasts until the beginning of its next turn. All creatures within 40 ft. of the deadly mandrake when it begins its scream, as well as those that end their turn within the area, must succeed on a DC 14 Fortitude save. (This save is only required once per scream.) Creatures under the effects of a fear effect take a -2 penalty on this save. Creatures that make their save are sickened for 1d2 rounds. Those that fail their save take 20 points of damage. If a screaming mandrake is damaged during a scream, it must make a Will save (DC 15 + damage taken) to maintain the scream; otherwise it ends. This is a sonic death effect. The save DC is Charisma-based."}],"description":"Deadly mandrakes are mischievous plant creatures believed to be part plant and part fey. They relish in playing tricks on those passing through their domain. These tricks are always more playful than harmful, at least to the deadly mandrake. Most such tricks take the form of lights or weird sounds meant to distract and confuse travelers.\n\nDeadly mandrakes sustain themselves on a diet of tree bark, leaves, and berries, and are most active during the day. At night they burrow into the ground to rest. Often times, a deadly mandrake burrows just far enough into the ground to cover its body, leaving the flower on top of its head exposed. Little else is known of these creatures thus far.\n\nDeadly mandrakes, despite their name, are generally inoffensive unless disturbed. If disturbed or forced into battle, deadly mandrakes use their death scream immediately in an attempt to end the confrontation as quickly as possible. Creatures that resist are subjected to sleep, lesser confusion, or hypnotism. They can attack with their claws if needed, but rarely do so.\n\nAny deadly mandrake killed has a 1% chance of returning in 1d4 days as a mandragora. If the deadly mandrake's body is doused with demon ichor (from a demon CR 6 or above), or a deadly mandrake is killed by a demon CR 6 or above, the chance rises to 10%. Using a deadly mandrake in the alchemical creation of a mandragora lowers the Craft (alchemy) DC from 25 to 20.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Death Dog","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"NE","size":"Medium","type":"magical beast","init":2,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":15,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":13,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +3 natural","hp":18,"hd":"2d10+4 (+3)","hdvalue":2,"fort":5,"ref":5,"will":2,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":40}],"melee":"2 bites +4 (1d6+1 plus rotting death and trip)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":13,"dexterity":15,"constitution":15,"intelligence":4,"wisdom":12,"charisma":6,"baseatk":2,"cmb":5,"cmd":17,"cmd_special":"+4 vs. trip","feats":[{"feat":"Toughness","type":"bonus"},{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":9},{"skill":"Stealth","value":6},{"skill":"Survival","value":1,"special":"+5 tracking by scent"}],"environments":["warm deserts"],"organizations":["hunt (2-4)","pack (5-10)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This creature appears to be a two-headed hound with rich black fur and yellow piercing eyes. Each head constantly drips a foul-smelling saliva.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Rotting Death","kind":"Su","details":"Curse and disease: Bite-bite; save Fort DC 13; onset 1 day; frequency 1/day; effect 1d6 Con; cure -. Rotting death is both a curses and disease and can only be cured if the curse is first removed, at which point the disease can be magically removed. The save DC is Dexterity-based."}],"description":"Death dogs are two-headed, mastiff-like hounds, nocturnal killing machines that hunt their prey without hesitation across the desert sands and wastelands. Death dog packs have been known to share territory with little friction, although they do engage in dominance battles in leaner times when hunting is difficult. Death dogs are strictly carnivores and often attack creatures much larger than themselves relying on their sheer numbers to bring down larger opponents.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Death Dog originally appeared in the First Edition Fiend Folio (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1981) and is used by permission."},{"name":"Diger","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Large","type":"ooze","init":0,"senses":[{"type":"blindsight","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":14,"ac_touch":9,"ac_flat_footed":14,"ac_mods":"+5 natural, -1 size","hp":31,"hd":"3d8+18","hdvalue":3,"fort":7,"ref":1,"will":10,"defensive_abilities":["transparent"],"immunities":["ooze traits"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":5},{"type":"fly","speed":30,"maneuverability":"poor"},{"type":"swim","speed":40}],"melee":"slam +3 (1d4+3 plus 1d4 acid plus paralysis)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"engulf","details":"DC 17, 1d4 acid and paralysis"},{"type":"paralysis","details":"1d6 rounds, DC 17"}],"strength":15,"dexterity":10,"constitution":23,"wisdom":1,"charisma":1,"baseatk":2,"cmb":14,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":27,"skills":[{"skill":"Fly","value":-6},{"skill":"Stealth","value":4},{"skill":"Swim","value":10}],"sq":[{"type":"limited flight"}],"environments":["any underground"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"A large pool of water is situated here. Floating near the center of it is a large reddish-colored gem.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Limited Flight","kind":"Ex","details":"By releasing naturally occurring helium from its body, a diger is able to fly for a number of rounds equal to 1 + 1/2 its current Constitution score (12 rounds for the average diger). After that, it must land and cannot fly again until it has rested for 2 minutes (20 rounds)."},{"type":"Transparent","kind":"Ex","details":"A diger is transparent in water. It takes a successful DC 15 Perception check to recognize a motionless diger for what it really is."}],"description":"Digers are semi-transparent oozes about 9 ft. in diameter that live in abandoned ruins or underground areas where they spend most of their time hunting for food. A diger's gem-like eye offers it a means to lure its prey in so it can attack. A character can attempt a DC 15 Perception check to realize the gem isn't real, but is in fact a part of the creature itself.\n\nAnyone with ranks in Appraise or Craft (gemcutting) can use one of those skills instead of Perception to notice.\n\nA diger attacks by hitting a foe with its slimy appendage, or by simply waiting, floating unnoticed until an opponent contacts it. Often, a diger lies at the bottom of a pool or stream where it is almost impossible to detect (+10 on Perception DC). Its gem-like eye appears to be a gemstone or jewel embedded in the ground under the water. An opponent that moves to grab the gemstone is immediately attacked by the diger.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Diger originally appeared in the First Edition module B3 Palace of the Silver Princess (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1981) and is used by permission."},{"name":"Dire Fox","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"animal","init":4,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":20,"ac_touch":14,"ac_flat_footed":16,"ac_mods":"+4 Dex, +6 natural","hp":34,"hd":"4d8+16","hdvalue":4,"fort":10,"ref":8,"will":2,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":50}],"melee":"bite +7 (1d6+4)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":16,"dexterity":19,"constitution":18,"intelligence":2,"wisdom":16,"charisma":10,"baseatk":3,"cmb":8,"cmd":20,"cmd_special":"+4 vs. trip","feats":[{"feat":"Great Fortitude"},{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":6},{"skill":"Perception","value":11},{"skill":"Stealth","value":13},{"skill":"Survival","value":8}],"environments":["cold forests","cold plains","temperate forests","temperate plains"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This giant fox has dark red fur and a mouthful of white glistening fangs.","source":"Tome of Horrors 4 (3pp)","description":"Dire foxes are larger and fiercer versions of the normal fox. They are solitary creatures whose hunting grounds can cover up to 10 square miles.\n\nDire foxes spend about one-third of their day hunting for food. They are excellent hunters and have no problem attacking and bringing down large prey. They are omnivorous and feast on a diet of rodents, dire rodents (such as dire rats), insects and giant insects, and various plants and herbs. If food is scarce, dire foxes live off of carrion until their food supply increases.\n\nMating season varies depending on climate. Dire foxes found in colder climates mate during the late winter and early spring months. Those found in warmer climates mate during the winter. This is the only time more than one dire fox will be encountered and such encounters consist of one male and at least one female (two females sometimes). If pups are present, there will be 2d6 of them. Young dire foxes reach maturity at about 10 months of age and leave the den to claim their own hunting territory which can be up to 250 miles away from their birthplace.\n\nDire foxes are about 5 ft. long and have yellowish-red or reddish-brown fur. A strip of black fur runs across their shoulders and down the center of their backs. The dire fox's underbelly is white more often than not, but a gray underbelly isn't uncommon. Dire foxes have thick, bushy tails with the same color fur as their body. The tail ends in a black or white bushy tip. The lower legs of a dire fox are either black or white and their eyes are yellow. A rare breed of dire fox has silver or silver-black fur.\n\nA dire fox prefers to sneak up on its prey and catch it unaware. When it spots a potential meal, the dire fox stands motionless, waiting for its target to move closer. When its prey is within range, it leaps out and attacks. If cornered, dire foxes fight to the death.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Dire Racoon","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Small","type":"animal","init":3,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":19,"ac_touch":14,"ac_flat_footed":16,"ac_mods":"+3 Dex, +5 natural, +1 size","hp":17,"hd":"2d8+8","hdvalue":2,"fort":7,"ref":6,"will":1,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30},{"type":"climb","speed":30}],"melee":"bite +5 (1d4+1 plus disease)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"disease"}],"strength":13,"dexterity":17,"constitution":18,"intelligence":2,"wisdom":16,"charisma":9,"baseatk":1,"cmb":-1,"cmd":12,"cmd_special":"+4 vs. trip","feats":[{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Climb","value":9},{"skill":"Perception","value":11},{"skill":"Stealth","value":11},{"skill":"Swim","value":5}],"environments":["temperate forests","temperate urban"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","family (1 adult plus 1-4 young)"],"treasure":"incidental","description_visual":"This animal is about the size of a large dog. Its fur is brownish-grey, and its bushy tail is ringed in black. Its paws and ears are black as well, and it has a black and white mask-like marking on its face.","group":"Racoon","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Disease","kind":"Ex","details":"Filth fever: Bite-injury; save Fort DC 15; onset 1d3 days; frequency 1 day; effect 1d3 Dex damage and 1d3 Con damage; cure 2 consecutive saves."}],"description":"Ever the opportunists, these giant procyonids tend to move out of their native habitat in the forests to the compost heaps and refuse piles of large cities. Food is easier to find there, and in good supply. Dire raccoons are not picky, and like their smaller cousins they are omnivorous. Rats, rotten food, and even the occasional corpse are all parts of an urban dire raccoon's diet. Because of its unsanitary lifestyle and questionable eating habits, dire raccoons that live in city trash heaps often carry filth fever.\n\nIn its natural habitat, a dire raccoon eats the same foods-although in greater amounts of course-as a normal raccoon. They are fond of shellfish, berries, and any small lizards or frogs they can catch. Dire raccoons have the same mating habits and rear the same number of young as their normal counterparts (see the raccoon entry).\n\nIn combat a dire raccoon engages in an intimidating display of shrill growls, stamping ft., and puffed up fur. Dire raccoons have sharp, strong teeth and use them well if cornered.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Electric Eel","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Large","type":"animal","subtypes":["aquatic"],"init":4,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":15,"ac_touch":13,"ac_flat_footed":11,"ac_mods":"+4 Dex, +2 natural, -1 size","hp":22,"hd":"4d8+4","hdvalue":4,"fort":5,"ref":8,"will":2,"immunities":["electricity"],"speed":[{"type":"swim","speed":40}],"melee":"bite +7 (1d8)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"electricity","details":"DC 13"}],"strength":10,"dexterity":18,"constitution":12,"intelligence":1,"wisdom":12,"charisma":2,"baseatk":3,"cmb":4,"cmd":18,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (bite)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":8},{"skill":"Swim","value":12}],"environments":["temperate aquatic","warm aquatic"],"organizations":["solitary","pair"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"The serpentine creature moves sinuously through the water, the tip of its tail sparking with an electrical discharge.","group":"Eel","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Electricity","kind":"Ex","details":"Once per hour, an electric eel can produce a jolt of electricity in a 10-foot radius centered on its body. Creatures within 5 ft. take 3d8 points of electricity damage. Those further away than 5 ft. but within 10 ft. take 2d8 points of electricity damage. Affected creatures can make a DC 13 Fortitude save to reduce the damage by half. Electric eels are immune to their own electrical attacks and that of other electric eels. The save DC is Constitution-based."}],"description":"Electric eels resemble snakes ranging from 9 to 20 ft. long and weighing 50 to 120 pounds. Its body is grayish-brown and lightens near the head. Small yellow splotches can be seen near the mouth. The electric eel is slimy to the touch and has no scales (the electric eel isn't a true eel, but a fish).\n\nAn electric eel prefers to discharge its electricity when it first enters combat. Those slain or rendered unconscious by the shock are devoured.\n\nShould any opponents survive the attack, the eel either flees (if outnumbered) or attacks its remaining foes with its bite.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Ethereal Rat","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Small","type":"magical beast","init":1,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":13,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":12,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +1 natural, +1 size","hp":11,"hd":"2d10","hdvalue":2,"fort":3,"ref":4,"will":1,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20},{"type":"climb","speed":20}],"melee":"bite +4 (1d4-2 plus ethereal poison)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"ethereal jaunt"}],"strength":6,"dexterity":13,"constitution":10,"intelligence":2,"wisdom":12,"charisma":2,"baseatk":2,"cmb":-2,"cmd":9,"cmd_special":"+4 vs. trip","feats":[{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Climb","value":6},{"skill":"Perception","value":6}],"environments":["any underground"],"organizations":["pack (6-11)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This creature is a large grayish-black rat about 2 ft. long.","group":"Rat","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Ethereal Poison","kind":"Ex","details":"Bite-injury; save Fort DC 13; frequency 1/round for 4 rounds; effect 1d4 Str, cure 1 save. The save DC is Constitution-based and includes a +2 racial bonus. A victim that reaches Strength 0 from an ethereal rat's poisonous bite becomes ethereal (as if affected by the etherealness spell). The victim remains in this state until at least 1 point of Strength damage is healed."},{"type":"Ethereal Jaunt","kind":"Su","details":"An ethereal rat can shift from the Ethereal to the Material Plane as a swift action and shift back again as a move action. The ability is otherwise identical with ethereal jaunt (caster level 15th)."}],"description":"Ethereal rats, like normal rats, they can be found just about anywhere.\n\nThey attack by biting a foe and then jumping to the Ethereal Plane. If badly wounded in battle an ethereal rat uses its ethereal jaunt ability to escape to the Ethereal Plane.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Executioner's Hood","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Tiny","type":"aberration","init":1,"senses":[{"type":"blindsense","modifier":"10 ft."},{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":16,"ac_touch":13,"ac_flat_footed":15,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +3 natural, +2 size","hp":11,"hd":"2d8+2","hdvalue":2,"fort":1,"ref":1,"will":4,"immunities":["sleep"],"weaknesses":["light sensitivity","vulnerability to alcohol"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":10},{"type":"climb","speed":5}],"melee":"slam +3 (1d4 plus grab)","space":"2-1/2 ft.","reach":"0 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"engulf"},{"type":"constrict","details":"1d4"},{"type":"strangle"}],"strength":10,"dexterity":12,"constitution":12,"intelligence":3,"wisdom":13,"charisma":10,"baseatk":1,"cmb":0,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":10,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Climb","value":8},{"skill":"Perception","value":8},{"skill":"Stealth","value":13}],"environments":["any underground"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"This creature resembles an executioner's hood constructed of blackish-gray leather. Two hollow eye holes are present in its form.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Engulf","kind":"Ex","details":"Though it is a Tiny monster, the hood can drop onto the head of a Medium or smaller creature as a standard action. If the hood's attack succeeds, it can immediately make a grapple check to grab its target and constrict. The hood's crawling tendrils wrap themselves tightly around its victim, seeking entry into the creature's ears, nose, and throat, making it difficult to remove. Any attacks against the hood deal half damage to the hood and its grappled target inside."},{"type":"Strangle","kind":"Ex","details":"A grappling executioner's hood entirely covers its opponent's head with an airtight grip. A creature that is grappled by the hood cannot speak or cast spells with verbal components and must hold its breath (see Suffocation) unless it doesn't speak or breathe through its head."},{"type":"Vulnerability to Alcohol","kind":"Ex","details":"Strong alcohol harms the hood. Every quart poured on it causes 1 point of damage. Four quarts of alcohol incapacitates it and forces it to release a grappled target."}],"description":"The executioner's hood is a deadly monster, black in color and 1 inch thick, that resembles an actual executioner's hood or a small black bag.\n\nIt has two eyeholes that can be used to see into or out of the monster (if some unfortunate soul happens actually to pick it up-or worse, put it on).\n\nThe executioner's hood is sometimes used to guard valuable belongings.\n\nIt does so unerringly as long as a constant supply of food is available.\n\nThe executioner's hood clings to the ceiling, waiting for prey to pass under it. When prey passes by, the hood drops and attempts to engulf the victim's head. Slain victims are slowly devoured by the hood.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Executioner's Hood originally appeared in the First Edition module EX1 Dungeonland (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1983) and later in the First Edition Monster Manual II (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1983) and is used by permission."},{"name":"Faerie Dragon","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"CG","size":"Tiny","type":"dragon","init":3,"senses":[{"type":"dragon senses"}],"ac":18,"ac_touch":16,"ac_flat_footed":14,"ac_mods":"+3 Dex, +1 dodge, +2 natural, +2 size","hp":22,"hd":"3d12+3","hdvalue":3,"fort":4,"ref":6,"will":5,"immunities":["magical paralysis","sleep"],"sr":14,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":10},{"type":"fly","speed":60,"maneuverability":"perfect"},{"type":"swim","speed":30}],"melee":"bite +8 (1d3-1)","space":"2-1/2 ft.","reach":"0 ft. (2-1/2 ft. with bite)","special_attacks":[{"type":"breath weapon","details":"5 ft. cone, Fort DC 12, euphoria 1d6 rounds"}],"spell_like_abilities_caster_level":3,"spell_like_abilities_concentration":4,"spell_like_abilities":[{"frequency":"3/day","spells":[{"spell":"greater invisibility","details":"self only"}]}],"spellsknown_caster":"sorcerer","spellsknown_caster_level":3,"spellsknown_concentration":4,"spellsknown":[{"spell_level":1,"spell_slots":6,"spells":[{"spell":"grease"},{"spell":"silent image"},{"spell":"sleep"}]},{"spell_level":0,"spell_slots":99,"spells":[{"spell":"dancing lights"},{"spell":"flare"},{"spell":"ghost sound"},{"spell":"mage hand"},{"spell":"open/close"}]}],"strength":9,"dexterity":17,"constitution":13,"intelligence":16,"wisdom":14,"charisma":16,"baseatk":3,"cmb":4,"cmd":14,"cmd_special":"+4 vs. trip","feats":[{"feat":"Acrobatic"},{"feat":"Dodge"},{"feat":"Weapon Finesse","type":"bonus"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":8},{"skill":"Bluff","value":9},{"skill":"Diplomacy","value":9},{"skill":"Fly","value":23},{"skill":"Knowledge (nature)","value":9},{"skill":"Perception","value":8},{"skill":"Sense Motive","value":8},{"skill":"Stealth","value":17},{"skill":"Swim","value":11},{"skill":"Use Magic Device","value":9}],"languages":["Common","Draconic","Elven","Sylvan","telepathy 100 ft."],"environments":["temperate forests","warm forests"],"organizations":["solitary","clan (2-8)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"This tiny dragon has delicate and brightly colored butterfly wings and a long, thin prehensile tail. Its scales are smooth and range in color from red to purple, with all colors of the spectrum falling in betwee n. Its eyes are bluish-green, and its backward curving horns are silver with gold flecks.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Breath Weapon","kind":"Su","details":"A faerie dragon's breath weapon is a 5-foot cone of pink gas. Creatures caught in the puff must succeed on a Fortitude save or experience extreme euphoria for 1d6 rounds. Affected creatures are staggered, sickened, and immune to fear effects for the duration of the euphoria."}],"description":"Faerie dragons are chaotic members of the dragon family and are believed to be distant cousins of the pseudodragon. Faerie dragons resemble tiny 1-2 foot long dragons. Female faerie dragons have a golden sheen to their coloring while males have a silver sheen. Faerie dragons love to play pranks on passersby and employ their spells to this end. Some faerie dragons spend months on end preparing for the day they can unleash their single grand practical joke or prank. Faerie dragons avoid combat and only attack if cornered or if their lair or young are in immediate danger. A faerie dragon attacks with its breath weapon, spells, and bite. Faerie Dragons as Familiars Although difficult to control, a faerie dragon can make for a powerful familiar. Spellcasters wishing to take a faerie dragon as a familiar must take the Improved Familiar feat and have an alignment within one step of chaotic good. The character must have an arcane spellcaster level of at least 7th before taking a faerie dragon as a familiar.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Faerie Dragon originally appeared in Dragon #62 (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1982) and later in the First Edition Monster Manual II (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1983) and is used by permission."},{"name":"Fetch","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"LE","size":"Medium","type":"undead","subtypes":["cold"],"init":2,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":14,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":13,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +2 natural","hp":22,"hd":"3d8+6 (+3)","hdvalue":3,"fort":3,"ref":3,"will":4,"immunities":["cold","undead traits"],"weaknesses":["vulnerability to fire"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"2 claws +6 (1d4+3 plus 1d4 cold)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"freezing touch"}],"strength":17,"dexterity":15,"intelligence":10,"wisdom":12,"charisma":15,"baseatk":2,"cmb":5,"cmd":17,"feats":[{"feat":"Toughness"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (claw)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Climb","value":9},{"skill":"Intimidate","value":8},{"skill":"Perception","value":8},{"skill":"Stealth","value":8}],"languages":["Common"],"environments":["cold plains","any hills","any mountains"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"A ragged-looking and rotting humanoid leaps from the snow, its filthy nails slashing through the frosty air. Its eyes are stark blue and its skin is pale white. Ice hangs from its scraggly hair.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Freezing Touch","kind":"Su","details":"A fetch's touch is supernaturally cold. It deals an extra 1d4 points of cold damage with each claw attack."}],"description":"When a murdered person is buried on frozen ground, it often returns from the grave as a fetch, an evil undead monster with a hatred of fire and the living. Fetches seek out living creatures that wander too close to their lair, kill them, and often bury them in hope that they rise from their frozen resting place as a fetch. Slain foes not buried are dragged to the fetch's lair and devoured. Fetches savor the flesh of humanoids and monstrous humanoids, in particular humans, elves, and centaurs.\n\nA fetch stands anywhere from 5 to 7 ft. tall and weighs between 100 and 250 pounds. Its clothes are tattered and worn with age and exposure.\n\nIts rotting flesh is drawn tight around its bones and flushed grayish-white.\n\nIts hair is scraggly and frozen and ice crystals cover its skin. A fetch's eyes are stark blue.\n\nA fetch is a ruthless opponent, slashing its foes with its wicked claws.\n\nFetches always fight to the death, but are smart enough to flee when presented with fire, if they cannot attack the opponent wielding the fire.\n\nIf a fetch flees from an encounter because of fire, it follows the one who presented the fire (lurking in the shadows, hiding in cover) and eventually exacts its revenge.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Medium Fire Crab","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"vermin","subtypes":["fire"],"init":1,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":16,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":15,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +5 natural","hp":19,"hd":"3d8+6","hdvalue":3,"fort":5,"ref":2,"will":1,"immunities":["fire","vermin traits"],"weaknesses":["cold vulnerability"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30},{"type":"swim","speed":20}],"melee":"2 claws +4 (1d4+2 plus grab and heat)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"constrict","details":"claw 1d4+2"},{"type":"heat","details":"+1d6 fire damage"}],"strength":14,"dexterity":12,"constitution":14,"wisdom":10,"charisma":2,"baseatk":2,"cmb":4,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":15,"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":4},{"skill":"Swim","value":10}],"organizations":["solitary","clutch (2-20)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"At various places in the planes, rifts of special tears appear and allow the mundane creatures of one plane to intermix with the creatures of other planes. On these occasions, it is most often not the terrible beasts such as demons or angels that pour through, but much less powerful (but not harmless) creatures mirroring the beasts of the natural realm.","group":"Animal Elemental","source":"Tome of Horrors 4 (3pp)","description":"Significantly larger than their cousins, the medium fire crab is a monstrosity slightly larger than a dog. The pincers, if they could extend fully (the joints of a fire crab almost turn in on themselves), would reach some 10 ft. or more.\n\nThe coloration of a medium fire crab varies from a bright red to nearly maroon in older specimens.\n\nFire crabs are omnivorous; they feed on other animals and any plant life that survives the fire crabs native environment. The fire crabs may survive temperatures lower than 100 degrees for 1 hour per point of Constitution, or run the risk of freezing.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Flame Drakeling","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Tiny","type":"dragon","subtypes":["fire"],"init":3,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":18,"ac_touch":15,"ac_flat_footed":15,"ac_mods":"+3 Dex, +3 natural, +2 size","hp":22,"hd":"3d12+3","hdvalue":3,"fort":4,"ref":6,"will":4,"immunities":["fire","paralysis","sleep"],"weaknesses":["vulnerability to cold"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20},{"type":"fly","speed":40,"maneuverability":"poor"}],"melee":"bite +8 (1d6-2 plus 1d4 fire)","space":"1/2 ft.","reach":"0 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"fire breath"},{"type":"heat generation"}],"strength":6,"dexterity":17,"constitution":12,"intelligence":6,"wisdom":13,"charisma":8,"baseatk":3,"cmb":4,"cmd":12,"feats":[{"feat":"Flyby Attack"},{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Fly","value":9},{"skill":"Intimidate","value":5},{"skill":"Perception","value":7},{"skill":"Stealth","value":17}],"languages":["Draconic"],"environments":["warm hills","warm mountains"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","band (3-6)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"This tiny creature looks like a miniature red dragon.","group":"Drakeling","source":"Tome of Horrors 4 (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Fire Breath","kind":"Su","details":"A flame drakeling can, as a standard action, spit a line of liquid fire to a range of 20 ft. A creature struck takes 2d6 points of fire damage (DC 12 Reflex half). Further, a creature that fails its save catches on fire and takes 1d6 points of fire damage each round until the flames are extinguished. Once a flame drakeling uses its fire breath, it cannot do so again for 1d4 rounds. The save DC is Constitution-based."},{"type":"Heat Generation","kind":"Su","details":"As a free action, a flame drakeling can increase its body temperature to extreme levels. Creatures attacking the flame drakeling with natural weapons or touching the flame drakeling take 1d4 points of fire damage each round of contact."}],"description":"Fire drakelings are believed to somehow be related to red dragons or flame drakes, but scholars have yet to find any connection linking the races together. Fire drakelings are generally docile creatures and make their lairs near active volcanoes. They are active during the day most of the time, feeding on insects and small game.\n\nFlame drakelings are about 12 inches long and weigh close to 70 pounds. Scales are varying hues of red, with darker scales running the length of the drakelings back and wings. Females and males are indistinguishable from one another. Flame drakelings mate twice per year with the female laying a small clutch of 4-6 eggs in the lair. eggs incubate for up to 8 months before hatching, and hatchlings reach maturity within 2 years. Flame drakelings live up to 150 years on average.\n\nFlame drakelings generally shun combat with larger foes, seeking refuge among the hills and mountains where their opponents can't reach them. If cornered or defending their young, flame drakelings attack with a vicious bite and surprisingly potent breath weapon.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Flind","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"LE","size":"Medium","type":"humanoid","subtypes":["gnoll"],"init":2,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":20,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":18,"ac_mods":"+5 armor, +2 Dex, +3 natural","hp":15,"hd":"2d8+6","hdvalue":2,"fort":6,"ref":2,"will":2,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20}],"melee":"flindbar +5 (1d6+4)","ranged":"longbow +3 (1d8/x3)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":19,"dexterity":14,"constitution":17,"intelligence":12,"wisdom":15,"charisma":12,"baseatk":1,"cmb":5,"cmb_special":"+2 vs. disarm with flindbar","cmd":17,"feats":[{"feat":"Power Attack"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Handle Animal","value":6},{"skill":"Intimidate","value":3},{"skill":"Perception","value":3}],"languages":["Common","Gnoll"],"environments":["any deserts","warm plains"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","hunting party (2-5 flinds and 1-2 hyenas)","band (10-100 adults plus 50% noncombatant children, 1 sergeant of 3rd level per 20 adults, 1 leader of 4th-6th level, and 5-8 hyenas)","tribe (20-200 plus 1 sergeant of 3rd level per 20 adults, 1 or 2 lieutenants of 4th or 5th level, 1 leader of 6th-8th level, 7-12 hyenas, and 4-7 hyaenodons)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"This creature resembles a bipedal hyena-like humanoid with dusky russet and brown fur. Its eyes are yellow and it wears dented and battle-worn scaled armor.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","description":"Flinds are a race closely related to gnolls. The two races share some strong similarities, and at first sight inexperienced adventurers could easily confuse the two. Flinds are much stockier than their lanky kin, much stronger and hardier, and are certainly more dangerous. It is unknown if flinds are a subspecies of the gnoll or a genetic anomaly produced among large gnoll packs. Flinds are often found among gnoll bands acting as leaders, as their strength and relatively superior intelligence puts them above their lesser brethren. Flind combat tactics are similar to those of gnolls. They prefer to strike from ambush, using terrain and concealment to their best advantage. They are most often seen acting in the role of leaders to gnoll bands, keeping discipline with a ruthless hand and little mercy.","as_character":[{"description":"Flind leaders are always fighters. Flind clerics usually worship the Demon Prince of Gnolls and can choose two of the following domains: Chaos, Evil, and Trickery. All flinds have the following racial traits +8 Str, +4 Dex, +6 Con, +2 Int, +4 Wis, +2 Cha: Flinds are exceptional gnolls, and excel in all areas over their lesser kin.","racial_traits":[{"type":"Darkvision","value":"Flinds cans see in the dark out to 60 ft."},{"type":"Armor","value":"Flinds have a +1 natural armor bonus."},{"type":"Racial Hit Dice","value":"A flind begins with two levels of humanoid, which provide 2d8 Hit Dice, a base attack bonus of +1, and base saving throw bonuses of Fort +3, Ref +0, and Will +0."},{"type":"Racial Skills","value":"A flind's humanoid levels give it skill rank equal to 2 x (2 + Int modifier)."},{"type":"Racial Feats","value":"A flind's humanoid levels give it one feat."},{"type":"Automatic Languages","value":"Gnoll."},{"type":"Bonus Languages","value":"Common, Draconic, Goblin, Orc."},{"type":"Weapon Proficiency","value":"Flinds are automatically proficient with the flindbar.\n\nFlindbar The wielder of a flindbar gains a +2 bonus on disarm attempts made with the weapon. Flindbars are otherwise identical to nunchaku. Anyone proficient with nunchaku is also proficient with flindbars."}]}],"ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Flind originally appeared in the First Edition Fiend Folio (© TSR/ Wizards of the Coast, 1981) and is used by permission.","gear":"scale mail armor, flindbar, longbow with 20 arrows"},{"name":"Forgotten One","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Diminutive","type":"fey","init":4,"senses":[{"type":"environmental awareness"},{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":19,"ac_touch":18,"ac_flat_footed":15,"ac_mods":"+4 Dex, +1 natural, +4 size","hp":10,"hd":"3d6","hdvalue":3,"fort":1,"ref":7,"will":6,"defensive_abilities":["environmental awareness"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20}],"melee":"shortspear +9 (1d2-4 plus poison)","ranged":"shortspear +9 (1d2-4 plus poison)","space":"2-1/2 ft.","reach":"0 ft.","spell_like_abilities_caster_level":3,"spell_like_abilities_concentration":3,"spell_like_abilities":[{"frequency":"at-will","spells":[{"spell":"blur"}]}],"strength":3,"dexterity":19,"constitution":11,"intelligence":14,"wisdom":17,"charisma":21,"baseatk":1,"cmb":1,"cmd":7,"feats":[{"feat":"Skill Focus (Knowledge [nature])"},{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":10,"special":"+6 jumping"},{"skill":"Climb","value":10},{"skill":"Craft (alchemy)","value":8},{"skill":"Escape Artist","value":10},{"skill":"Knowledge (nature)","value":11},{"skill":"Perception","value":9},{"skill":"Sense Motive","value":9},{"skill":"Stealth","value":22},{"skill":"Survival","value":7}],"languages":["Common","Elven","Sylvan"],"sq":[{"type":"forgetful presence"}],"environments":["any forests"],"organizations":["troop (2-8)","clan (10-100)"],"treasure":"incidental","description_visual":"This is a small 1-foot tall creature with pointed ears, slanted eyes, and long lithe limbs. Its hair is brightly colored and decorated with leaves and twigs and it is dressed in greenish-brown clothes. It carries a tiny spear in its hands.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Environmental Awareness","kind":"Ex","details":"The very earth speaks to a forgotten one. A forgotten one has the benefits of tremorsense and blindsight within a 100-foot radius. It cannot be flanked or caught flat-footed."},{"type":"Poison Use","kind":"Ex","details":"Forgotten Ones are skilled in the use of poison and never risk accidentally poisoning themselves. Forgotten ones use a mixture of bee and spider venom on their spears that causes severe itching and soreness. Forgotten One Poison-injury; save Fort DC 11; frequency 1/hour for 24 hours; effect -1 (non-cumulative) penalty on attack rolls, skill checks, and saving throws, if the target fails at least 8 saves it also takes 1 point of Con damage; cure 1 save. Humanoids and monstrous humanoids have a 5% chance of being severely allergic to forgotten one poison. The effects of a failed save are as above, but the penalty is -2 rather than -1, and 1d3 Con damage."},{"type":"Forgetful Presence","kind":"Su","details":"Three times per day, a forgotten one can cause any creature within 20 ft. of it to forget an encounter with it as per a modify memory spell with the restriction that it can only remove memories specifically dealing with the forgotten one. Opponents that succeed on a DC 16 Will save avoid the effects. Opponents that make the save are immune to the forgetful presence of that forgotten one for 24 hours. The save DC is Charisma-based."}],"description":"Forgotten ones are a sprite race related to pixies. These wee folk are natural spies and trackers, and are routinely employed by powerful forest-guarding fey creatures such as unicorns, dryads, and nymphs. It isn't uncommon for travelers in a fey forest to be followed by scores of forgotten ones, all moving unnoticed through the treetops as quickly and quietly as squirrels. While some forgotten ones keep track of the interlopers, others send messages back and forth to alert more powerful fey guardians to the presence of outsiders.\n\nA forgotten one is about a foot tall with pointed ears, slanted eyes, and long, nimble limbs. Most forgotten ones weave twigs and leaves into their hair for decoration and to help conceal themselves in the treetops.\n\nRangers are druids are especially fond of the forgotten ones, and often use them as intermediaries between themselves and the greater Fey guardians of the forest.\n\nForgotten ones try to avoid combat with anything larger than themselves-they are well aware of their own limitations. If hard pressed or ordered into combat by a greater fey creature to defend their forest, forgotten ones will fight with courage not often attributed to creatures of such small size.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Forlarren","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"NE","size":"Medium","type":"fey","init":2,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":18,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":16,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +6 natural","hp":13,"hd":"3d6+3","hdvalue":3,"fort":2,"ref":5,"will":4,"dr":"5/cold iron","speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"2 slams +3 (1d4+1)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"heat metal"}],"strength":12,"dexterity":15,"constitution":12,"intelligence":4,"wisdom":13,"charisma":8,"baseatk":1,"cmb":2,"cmd":14,"feats":[{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"},{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":10},{"skill":"Stealth","value":8},{"skill":"Survival","value":4}],"languages":["Forlarren","Sylvan"],"environments":["any temperate land"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This creature resembles a hairless satyr. A small ruff of dark hair covers the back of its head. Small horns protrude above its eyes. Its hands end in wicked claws and its face shows a twisted, leering visage.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Heat Metal","kind":"Su","details":"Once per day, by making a melee touch attack, a forlarren can heat metal (as the spell, caster level 6th, DC 12 Will save). Once the affected metal reaches the searing stage (after 3 rounds), it remains at that stage until the forlarren is killed, moves more than 100 ft. from the target, or dismisses the effect (as a free action). Once the effect has been dismissed, the metal slowly returns to its normal temperature (just as in the spell). The save DC is Constitution-based."}],"description":"The powers of a nymph are such that she can enchant and seduce nearly any creature that has the capacity to know beauty. Some say that even the great and terrible daemons are not immune to a nypmh's charms. The forlarren, they say, is the proof. The forlarren is a lonely creature that feels cursed by its own existence. They detest themselves and everything they see, consumed by hatred of life itself. Such is their rage that they seek to vent their ire on good and evil alike.\n\nA forlarren stands 6 ft. tall and weighs about 160 pounds.\n\nThe forlarren attacks with its fists, using them to pummel an opponent. It focuses on a single opponent in combat and attacks until it or its opponent is slain. If a forlarren succeeds in killing an opponent, the kindly traits of its fey mother surface and it shows profound remorse. It ceases combat, if possible, or flees if other opponents insist on continuing the fight. Should its opponents allow combat to end, the forlarren may offer its solace to the surviving companions amid wails and sobs. After a few days, however, the dominant evil nature of its fiendish father resurfaces and the forlarren once more attacks all creatures on sight-including those it had previously befriended.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Forlarren originally appeared in the First Edition Fiend Folio (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1981) and is used by permission."},{"name":"Frost Man","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"LE","size":"Medium","type":"humanoid","subtypes":["cold"],"init":1,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":15,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":14,"ac_mods":"+2 armor, +1 Dex, +2 natural","hp":22,"hd":"4d8+4","hdvalue":4,"fort":5,"ref":2,"will":1,"immunities":["cold"],"weaknesses":["vulnerability to fire"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"morningstar +3 (1d8)","ranged":"longbow +4 (1d8/x3)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"ice blast","details":"3/day, 30 ft. cone, DC 13 half, 3d6 cold"}],"strength":10,"dexterity":12,"constitution":12,"intelligence":10,"wisdom":11,"charisma":11,"baseatk":3,"cmb":3,"cmd":14,"feats":[{"feat":"Blind-Fight"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Survival)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":3},{"skill":"Stealth","value":5},{"skill":"Survival","value":7}],"languages":["Common","Frost Man"],"environments":["cold hills","cold underground"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"This creature appears to be a human dressed in loosely fitting animal skins and furs. It wears a patch over one eye and its hair is long and unkempt. A short, rough beard of dark hair covers its jaw.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Ice Blast","kind":"Su","details":"Three times per day as a standard action, a frost man can release a blast of freezing mist in a 30-foot cone from the eye underneath its eye patch. A frost man can remove its eye patch as a free action. A creature in the area takes 3d6 points of cold damage (DC 13 Reflex for half). The save DC is Constitution-based."}],"description":"Frost men are hunters that make their home in the cold regions of the world. Each carries his personal belongings in small sacks and takes them wherever he goes. A frost man's body radiates cold out to 30 ft., though not enough to deal damage. There is much speculation on the society of frost men. Other beings only encounter them as lone males. Speculation suggests that there must be villages somewhere with women and children, perhaps buried deep in cold mountain caves. Tribes that are aware of frost men fear them greatly for their deadly talent and refer to them as \"ice demons.\" Frost men appear in the outside world occasionally and wish only to go about their unknown business undisturbed. Anyone bothering them can expect to be attacked. A frost man usually opens combat with its ice blast before moving in to kill off anything not subsequently frozen to death.","as_character":[{"description":"Most frost man leaders are fighters or fighter/rogues. Sorcerers, rogues, and clerics are not uncommon among frost men. Frost men clerics can choose any two of the following domains: Earth, Evil, Law, Water. Frost men characters possess the following racial traits.","racial_traits":[{"type":"Ability Score Bonuses","value":"+2 Dex, +2 Con: Frost men have good balance and are quite hardy."},{"type":"Darkvision","value":"A frost man can see in the dark out to 60 ft."},{"type":"Immune","value":"Cold."},{"type":"Weakness","value":"Vulnerability to fire."},{"type":"Armor","value":"Frost men have a +2 natural armor bonus."},{"type":"Racial Hit Dice","value":"A frost man begins with four levels of humanoid, which provide 4d8 Hit Dice, a base attack bonus of +3, and base saving throw bonuses of Fort +4, Ref +1, and Will +1."},{"type":"Special Attacks","value":"Ice blast."},{"type":"Racial Skills","value":"A frost man's humanoid levels give it skill points equal to 4 x (2 + Int modifier)."},{"type":"Racial Feats","value":"A frost man's humanoid levels give it two feats."},{"type":"Automatic Languages","value":"Common, Frost Man."},{"type":"Bonus Languages","value":"Draconic, Elven, Giant, Gnoll, Orc."}]}],"ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Frost Man originally appeared in the First Edition Fiend Folio (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1981) and is used by permission.","gear":"leather armor, morningstar, longbow, 30 arrows; Other Gear tunic, sack"},{"name":"Fungus Man","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Small","type":"plant","init":2,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":15,"ac_touch":13,"ac_flat_footed":13,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +2 natural, +1 size","hp":16,"hd":"3d8+3","hdvalue":3,"fort":4,"ref":3,"will":2,"dr":"5/slashing or piercing","immunities":["poison","plant traits"],"resists":[{"type":"cold","value":5}],"weaknesses":["vulnerable to fire"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20}],"melee":"slam +5 (1d3-2)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"spore cloud"}],"strength":6,"dexterity":15,"constitution":13,"intelligence":9,"wisdom":12,"charisma":10,"baseatk":2,"cmb":3,"cmd":11,"feats":[{"feat":"Agile Maneuvers"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"},{"feat":"Weapon Finesse","type":"bonus"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":9},{"skill":"Stealth","value":10}],"environments":["any underground"],"organizations":["pod (4-16)","branch (17-30)","colony (30+, plus a fungus man king)"],"treasure":"incidental","description_visual":"These small, non-aggressive plant-men appear to be mobile toadstools, with brightly colored caps, stubby arms and thick, trunk-like legs. They don't speak, but they hold up their hands in a sign of peace or supplication.","source":"Tome of Horrors 4 (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Spore Cloud","kind":"Ex","details":"Once per round as a standard action, a fungus man can release a cloud of noxious spores. All living creatures within 5 ft. must make a DC 12 Fortitude save or become nauseated for 1d4+1 rounds. The save DC is Constitution-based. This is a poison effect, and once a creature successfully saves it is immune to the spore cloud of that particular fungus man for 24 hours."}],"description":"Fungus men are an extremely peaceful and easy-going race of intelligent plants. They are bipedal, in a squat, humanoid fashion. The fungus men are sexless, and reproduce by means of spore clouds. They lack the appendages for fine manipulation of objects, but are quite dexterous in their own way. The fungus men are fiercely loyal to their fungus man king, and attack immediately if it is threatened.\n\nFungus men don't communicate by verbal means; they have learned to deal with outsiders by using hand gestures and supplicating movements. They communicate with each other by pheromones and spores release.\n\nFungus men cohabitate in enclaves throughout the underdark, farming lichen and mosses to trade with other races for soil and excrement (their sources of food). Some fungus man colonies keep \"domesticated\" giant lizards available to replenish their dung fields and for pack animal uses. Fungus man colonies are not hostile towards each other; neither do they work together in any societal fashion.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"Originally appearing in Rappan Athuk Reloaded (© Necromancer Games, 2006)"},{"name":"Fyr","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Small","type":"fey","init":1,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":15,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":14,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +3 natural, +1 size","hp":9,"hd":"2d6+2","hdvalue":2,"fort":4,"ref":4,"will":4,"dr":"5/cold iron","sr":15,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"butt +3 (1d4+1) or longsword +4 (1d6+1)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","spell_like_abilities_caster_level":9,"spell_like_abilities_concentration":10,"spell_like_abilities":[{"frequency":"1/day","spells":[{"spell":"pass without trace"}]},{"frequency":"3/day","spells":[{"spell":"warp wood"}]},{"frequency":"4/day","spells":[{"spell":"mass charm animal","details":"this functions as charm animal, but allows the fyr to charm up to 4 HD of animals"}]}],"strength":12,"dexterity":13,"constitution":12,"intelligence":12,"wisdom":13,"charisma":13,"baseatk":1,"cmb":1,"cmd":12,"feats":[{"feat":"Alertness"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Appraise","value":5},{"skill":"Bluff","value":5},{"skill":"Craft (jewelry)","value":6},{"skill":"Handle Animal","value":7},{"skill":"Knowledge (nature)","value":6},{"skill":"Perception","value":7},{"skill":"Sense Motive","value":7},{"skill":"Stealth","value":9,"special":"+17 in above-ground natural environments"},{"skill":"Survival","value":2}],"languages":["Common","Sylvan","speak with animals"],"sq":[{"type":"weapon attunement"}],"environments":["temperate forests","temperate mountains"],"organizations":["solitary","pack (1 dire goat or 1 brown bear or 1d2 sheep or 1 ram or 1d4 badgers or 1 dire badger)","band (2-5 plus 1d3 dire badgers)","troop (6-11 fyrs plus 1d4 dire goats or 1d6 black bears or 2d6 sheep or 1d6 rams or 2d4 deer)"],"treasure":"50% coins; double goods (jewelry only); 50% items","description_visual":"This small, goat-headed humanoid has large backward curving horns, goat-like legs, a small bushy tail, and a human torso. Except its arms and hands, thick brown fur covers its entire body. It is heavily adorned with rings, necklaces, bracelets, earrings, and other such trinkets.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Weapon Attunement","kind":"Su","details":"Fyrs have a mystical ability to attune themselves with any weapon they wield. A weapon held by a fyr functions as a masterwork item (gaining a +1 bonus to attack rolls) as long as the fyr wields it. This bonus doesn't stack with the enhancement bonus gained from magical weapons, or the bonus gained from a masterwork weapon. This bonus is included in the statistics block above."}],"description":"Fyr are satyr-like creatures that make their homes in the mountainous wilds and dense forests in well-secluded places such as caves or caverns or under a covering of thick branches and giant leaves. They are a solitary and nomadic race, rarely staying in one place for much longer than a few months before moving on. Though always on the move, a fyr can acclimate itself to its surroundings very quickly. Most encounters are with a solitary creature or a solitary creature and its animal companions.\n\nFyrs are on good terms with gnomes, druids, treants, elves, and halflings, and often trade goods with them or aid them in times of need. Fyr are master jewelers, and their trinkets and baubles are highly sought after by some of the more civilized races that appreciate fine jewelry. These fey tolerate humans and dwarves and likewise trade with them when they near a human settlement or dwarven stronghold. Fyr dislike orcs, goblins, bugbears, and other goblinoids, and avoid contact with them.\n\nFyrs prefer to avoid combat if possible, but if their homeland or comrades are threatened, they attack. Fyrs use their natural ability to hide themselves in their terrain and attack from surprise. Most of the time a fyr attacks by butting with its horns, but some prefer a \"less animalistic\" form of combat and use weapons in combat. Favored weapons include a small longsword, club, hand axe, and dagger. A fyr doesn't hesitate to retreat if the tide of battle turns against it and usually employs pass without trace so it can escape without being tracked. If encountered with its animal companions, the fyr directs them in combat against its foes.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Ghoul Wolf","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"NE","size":"Medium","type":"undead","init":2,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":14,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":12,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +2 natural","hp":18,"hd":"4d8","hdvalue":4,"fort":1,"ref":3,"will":6,"defensive_abilities":["channel resistance +2"],"immunities":["undead traits"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":50}],"melee":"bite +7 (1d6+4 plus paralysis plus trip)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"paralysis","details":"1d4+2 rounds, DC 12, elves are immune to this effect"}],"strength":17,"dexterity":15,"intelligence":6,"wisdom":14,"charisma":10,"baseatk":3,"cmb":6,"cmd":18,"cmd_special":"+4 vs. trip","feats":[{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (bite)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":9},{"skill":"Stealth","value":6},{"skill":"Survival","value":2,"special":"+6 tracking by scent"},{"skill":"Swim","value":5}],"environments":["any"],"organizations":["solitary","pack (7-16)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This creature resembles a wolf with matted dark fur torn away in places.  Its flesh is sickly gray where its fur is torn away. Its eyes are stark white.","group":"Wolf","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","description":"Ghoul wolves are carnivorous undead wolves that delight in hunting living creatures, catching them, and tearing them to shreds. These creatures are most often found haunting desolate moors and marshes.\n\nGhoul wolves hunt in packs, surrounding their prey and circling as they move in for the kill.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Giant Clam","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Large","type":"vermin","subtypes":["aquatic"],"init":-5,"senses":[{"type":"blindsight","modifier":"30 ft."}],"ac":14,"ac_touch":4,"ac_flat_footed":14,"ac_mods":"-5 Dex, +10 natural, -1 size","hp":26,"hd":"4d8+8","hdvalue":4,"fort":6,"ref":-4,"will":1,"defensive_abilities":["camouflage"],"immunities":["mind-affecting effects"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":5}],"space":"10 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"acid"},{"type":"engulf"}],"strength":20,"dexterity":1,"constitution":15,"wisdom":10,"charisma":9,"baseatk":3,"cmb":9,"cmd":14,"environments":["temperate aquatic","warm aquatic"],"organizations":["solitary","cluster (2-10)"],"treasure":"incidental","description_visual":"A large brightly colored shell shifts in the currents. Seemingly split or divided down the middle, its interior is a dazzling golden brown.","group":"Clam","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Acid","kind":"Ex","details":"A giant clam has a slow-acting acid that it uses to break down organic matter trapped in its interior. An engulfed creature takes 1d2 points of acid damage each round."},{"type":"Engulf","kind":"Ex","details":"As a standard action, a giant clam can attempt to pull a creature up to one size smaller than itself that is within reach into its interior. An opponent can make an attack of opportunity against the clam, but if it does so it isn't entitled to a saving throw. An opponent that doesn't attempt an attack of opportunity must succeed on a DC 17 Reflex save or be pulled into the clam's interior. An engulfed creature is subject to the clam's acid, and is considered to be grappled and trapped within its body. The save DC is Strength-based. A giant clam can be forced open by making an opposed Strength check against the clam's Strength check. Otherwise, it opens on its own in 1d4 hours."},{"type":"Camouflage","kind":"Ex","details":"The rough shell of a giant clam is usually draped with barnacles, anemones, and bits of coral, which help it to blend in with its environment. Creatures must succeed on a DC 20 Perception check to notice a giant clam. Anyone with ranks in Survival or Knowledge (any water- or sea-related skill) can use one of those skills instead of Perception to notice the giant clam."}],"description":"Giant clams are generally found in coastal waters no deeper than 60 ft. from the surface of the water. Many species of giant clams subsist strictly on a diet of sunlight, and as such are never found in deeper waters where sunlight cannot reach. Such giant clams are generally found in shallow seas or attached to coral reefs near the surface. Some species of giant clams feed not only on sunlight but also on what they can filter from the water, usually small plants and animals, and sometimes the occasional swimmer. A giant clam moves by pushing out a small \"foot\" and sliding itself along. Giant clams simply wait until an unsuspecting opponent swims too close to its current position. When a creature at least one size smaller than the clam swims within reach, it sucks the prey into its interior and clamps shut. It slowly digests its meal and expels any indigestible material (such as metal and stone) into the surrounding water. Air-breathers trapped by a giant clam face the danger of drowning as well.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Giant Clam originally appeared in the First Edition module EX2 Land Beyond the Magic Mirror (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1983) and is used by permission."}];
